      "body": [
        {
          "type": "TextualBody",
          "value": "<p></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">Concerning Reuben who holds Simeon\u2019s IOU for a debt to be paid in instalments, some of which has become due. Reuben demanded payment of what is due from Simeon, but he claimed to be destitute and swore with an oath by the Torah that he has no possessions and cannot pay it. What is his obligation afterwards? And if Reuben transgresses by going out and suing his fellow litigant in a gentile court, with the intention of causing him harm and damage and defaming and harassing him, and does not abide by what Jewish law requires of him concerning the matter that is between them, what is incumbent upon him?</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"></p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\">Answer: As long as Simeon claims under oath to be destitute, there is no way at all that Reuben can hinder him. And if Reuben transgresses and deviates from what the ruling of Jewish law requires of him, he is to be placed under the ban. Reuben must make restitution to Simeon and compensate him for any damages he causes him in the gentile court not required by Jewish law.</p>",
          "format": "text/html"
        }
      ],
      "motivation": [
        "sc:supplementing",
